./seed/analysis-2026-09-01/report.md:137:1. *Distance to the ideal.* Given a sparse $f\in R_N$, estimate $\langle f|P_0|f\rangle/\|f\|^2=\mathrm{dist}_{\mathrm{BW}}(f,I_N)^2/\|f\|^2$ to additive $\epsilon$: cost $\tilde O(T/\epsilon)$ with amplitude estimation. This decides "$f\in I_N$ or $f$ is $\epsilon$-far" but is fundamentally an *estimation* problem, and its hardness (Conjecture 8.1) is stated as such. Exact membership is EXPSPACE-complete (Mayr–Meyer) only for unbounded degree; at fixed $N$ it is linear algebra of dimension $\binom{N+n}{n}$, and the advantage is exponential in $n$ only in the promise regime.
./seed/analysis-2026-09-01/report.md:170:- **Prior art on HHL for Macaulay matrices.** Chen–Gao (2018/2022) run an HHL solver on a Macaulay linear system over $\mathbb{C}$ encoding a Boolean system, with cost polynomial in the condition number; Ding–Gheorghiu–Gilyén–Hallgren–Li (Quantum 2023) prove that this condition number is exponentially large unless the solution has Hamming weight $O(\log n)$, so Grover wins. Their system is a different Macaulay matrix (a solve for a particular vector) and there is no established reduction between their condition number and the quantities here; but the lesson is the same: in the hard instances the cost hides in a normalisation, not in a spectral gap, and any claim here must survive that test. The related "bosonic quantum computational complexity" framework (Chabaud–Joseph–Mehraban–Motamedi 2024) concerns unbounded-particle bosonic models and is different from the fixed-$N$ symmetric sector here.
